<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lillibullero</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">tune first published <a href="/years/1686">1686</a>; Wharton's words <a href="/years/1686">1686</a>; broadsides and wide popularity <a href="/years/1688">1688</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The melody of &quot;Lillibullero&quot; is attributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-purcell">Henry Purcell</a>, though it is unclear whether he composed it or arranged it; it was first published in The Delightful Companion, John Playford's method book for the recorder, in 1686, and no original <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> survives.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In 1686 Thomas, Lord Wharton wrote words to the tune satirising King James II's appointment of the Catholic Richard Talbot, 1st Earl of Tyrconnell, as Lord Deputy of Ireland. The song is a mocking conversation between two Irishmen about Talbot's coming and what it would mean for the Protestants. Lilliburlero had been the watchword of Irish Catholics in the Irish Rebellion of 1641.</p><p>The song made little impression at first. When James II began moving Irish regiments to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in 1688, broadsides of the words were printed, and by October it was sung in the streets of English towns and in the barracks to mock the arriving Irish troops. The Whigs all but adopted it as their anthem during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glorious-revolution">Glorious Revolution</a>; after William of Orange invaded the next month even the palace guards were said to sing it, and a second part appeared as he advanced. Wharton boasted that he had sung a deluded prince out of three kingdoms.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The refrain Lillibullero bullen a la follows every line. The verses mock Talbot's filling of the Irish army with Catholic officers and the hopes of Irish Catholics for the return of their lands and power, which roused Protestant fears of a massacre like that of 1641. The refrain has been read as garbled Irish, as a reference to the fleur de lis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, or to the astrologer William Lilly; Professor Breandán Ó Buachalla took it to mean that Lilly would be proved right and the day would be ours.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>By 17 November 1688 an anti-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> parody, A New Song Upon the Hogen Mogens, was already circulating. The tune carried the highwayman Macheath's song in John Gay's The Beggar's Opera of 1728, the Ulster Protestant song Protestant Boys and the drinking song Nottingham Ale. Laurence Sterne printed its score in Tristram Shandy, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolfgang-amadeus-mozart">Wolfgang Amadeus Mozart</a> appears to have adapted it for the first movement of his Piano Sonata No. 11 of 1783. Adopted by the BBC's Second World War programme Into Battle, it served for decades as the interval signal of the BBC World Service before a new theme was introduced in 2008, and it is the regimental march of the Royal Electrical and Mechanical Engineers.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lillibullero” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…European congregations kept two older tunes for parts of the year: one for the Omer weeks between Passover and Shavuot, which has been compared to &quot;<b>Lillibullero</b>&quot; and to Mozart's Marriage of Figaro and resembles late 17th-century German folk song, and one for the Three Weeks before Tisha b'Av.</blockquote><a href="/songs/lekha-dodi">Lekha Dodi</a></li><li><blockquote>The melody of &quot;<b>Lillibullero</b>&quot; is attributed to Henry Purcell, though it is unclear whether he composed it or arranged it; it was first published in The Delightful Companion, John Playford's method book for the recorder, in 1686, and no original manuscript survives.</blockquote><a href="/songs/lillibullero">Lillibullero</a></li></ul></section>
